## Code organization

Each `.py` file contains its own dependencies and all required code; it does not
import another course file or load the Mathematica notebooks. There are three
parts: **physics functions**, **controls and their connection to the physics**, and
**visualization**. Most student edits belong in the physics functions.

The eigenstate expansion is evaluated once per parameter change. Plotly handles
Play/Pause and time scrubbing in the browser, using precomputed states. Changing
playback speed changes the presentation, not the physical evolution. Plotly's
selected time is a display control and is not sent back to Python.

The optional Zeno curve is the probability of an all-YES measurement history.
The site-probability bars continue to show the unmeasured experiment; they do not
show a conditioned or outcome-averaged measured state.

See [TEACHING.md](TEACHING.md) for lecture activities, short coding tasks,
AI prompts, and instructor answers.

## Optional local use

No installation is needed on students' laptops when they use molab. For local use,
install [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) and run one of:

```bash
uv run --with marimo --with numpy --with plotly marimo edit spin_dynamics.py
uv run --with marimo --with numpy --with plotly marimo edit chain_dynamics.py
```

Dependencies are also declared in the notebooks' script headers. The files were
validated with marimo 0.25.0 and Plotly 7.1.0. See the official
[molab GitHub launcher](https://molab.marimo.io/github),
[marimo plotting guide](https://docs.marimo.io/guides/working_with_data/plotting/),
and [Plotly animation documentation](https://plotly.com/python/animations/).
